**In class (`Lec-6/programming_exercise.cpp`):** write `struct Order { id, price, qty, buy/sell }` and `struct Trader { id, name }`. Five traders in a loop each send 100 orders per second. Version 1 must **promise to use no heap memory**, verified with **`strace`**. Version 2 is the heap version, for comparison.

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <cstdint>
#include <thread>
enum class Side : uint8_t { Buy, Sell };
struct Order  { uint64_t id; int64_t price; uint32_t qty; Side side; };  // 24 bytes
struct Trader { uint32_t id; char name[16]; };      // fixed char array, not std::string

int main() {
    std::array<Trader, 5> traders{{{1,"A"},{2,"B"},{3,"C"},{4,"D"},{5,"E"}}};
    std::array<Order, 100> batch;                    // reused every second, on the stack
    uint64_t next_id = 0;
    while (true) {
        for (auto& t : traders)
            for (auto& o : batch)
                o = Order{++next_id, 10'000 + int64_t(next_id % 50), 10,
                          (next_id & 1) ? Side::Buy : Side::Sell};
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
    }
}
```

```bash
g++ -O2 stack_orders.cpp -o s && strace -f -e trace=brk,mmap,munmap ./s
# after start-up (loader + libc setup) there should be NO brk/mmap calls inside the loop
```

The heap version (`std::vector<Order>` created inside the loop, `new Trader`, a `std::string` name) shows allocator activity, and `ltrace`/`perf` show `malloc`/`free` on every iteration. Even when `malloc` makes no syscall, it costs time and adds jitter.

Watch out for hidden allocations: `std::string` beyond SSO (more than 15 characters in libstdc++), `std::function` with big captures, `std::vector` growth, `std::map` and `std::unordered_map` nodes, exceptions, and iostream buffers (allocated once).

**In class (`Lec-6/vector.cpp`):**

```cpp
class Vector {
    uint32_t size;  int64_t* arr;  uint32_t index;
public:
    Vector(uint32_t sz) : size(sz), arr(new int64_t[sz]), index(0) {}
    void push_back(int64_t v) { arr[index++] = v; }   // no capacity check!
    void print() const;
};
int main() { uint32_t N; std::cin >> N; Vector obj{N};
             for (int i = 0; i < 2000; i++) { obj.push_back(12); obj.push_back(13); obj.push_back(15); } }
```

It shows the core idea (a heap buffer plus a count, and pre-sizing from input), but as written:

1. **Heap buffer overflow.** 6000 elements are pushed. If `N < 6000`, the writes go past the end of the allocation: UB that corrupts the allocator's metadata.
2. **Leak.** There is no destructor, so `arr` is never `delete[]`d.
3. **Rule of three.** Once you add a destructor, the implicit copy constructor copies the *pointer*, so two `Vector`s would `delete[]` the same buffer (a double free). You need a copy constructor and copy assignment (and move operations, Rule of 5).

A minimal correct growing version:

```cpp
class Vector {
    int64_t* data_ = nullptr; size_t size_ = 0, cap_ = 0;
public:
    explicit Vector(size_t cap) : data_(new int64_t[cap]), cap_(cap) {}
    ~Vector() { delete[] data_; }
    Vector(const Vector&) = delete;            // or implement a deep copy
    Vector& operator=(const Vector&) = delete;
    void push_back(int64_t v) {
        if (size_ == cap_) grow();             // amortised O(1)
        data_[size_++] = v;
    }
private:
    void grow() {
        size_t nc = cap_ ? cap_ * 2 : 8;       // geometric growth
        int64_t* nd = new int64_t[nc];
        std::copy(data_, data_ + size_, nd);
        delete[] data_; data_ = nd; cap_ = nc;
    }
};
```

**The low-latency takeaway:** the class version's real idea, *size the buffer once up front from known input*, is exactly `std::vector::reserve`. Growth is a latency spike (allocate, copy n elements, free), so do it at start-up and never in the hot loop.

An **object pool** (fixed-size free list) pre-allocates N slots of one type and hands them out in O(1) with no syscall:

```cpp
template <typename T, size_t N>
class Pool {
    union Slot { Slot* next; alignas(T) unsigned char storage[sizeof(T)]; };
    std::array<Slot, N> slots_;      // one contiguous block (could be a static or a huge page)
    Slot* free_ = nullptr;
public:
    Pool() {                          // thread all slots onto the free list once
        for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) { slots_[i].next = free_; free_ = &slots_[i]; }
    }
    template <class... Args> T* create(Args&&... a) {
        if (!free_) return nullptr;   // pool exhausted: a design-time sizing decision
        Slot* s = free_; free_ = s->next;               // pop: O(1), two instructions
        return new (s->storage) T(std::forward<Args>(a)...);  // placement new
    }
    void destroy(T* p) {
        p->~T();
        Slot* s = reinterpret_cast<Slot*>(p);
        s->next = free_; free_ = s;                     // push: O(1)
    }
};
Pool<Order, 1'000'000> orders;   // sized for the worst day, allocated once
```

| | `new`/`delete` per order | Pre-allocated pool |
|---|---|---|
| Alloc cost | ~20–100 ns, occasionally µs | a few ns (pop from the free list) |
| Syscalls or page faults during trading | possible | none (pre-faulted) |
| Thread safety | general-purpose locks or tcache | single-threaded by design (or one pool per thread) |
| Fragmentation | yes, over time | none: all slots are the same size |
| Locality | objects scattered | objects packed in one block |
| Flexibility | any size, unbounded | one size, fixed capacity |

The trade-off is that capacity must be chosen up front, and objects of different sizes need different pools (or size classes).

Every standard container takes an allocator parameter, `std::vector<T, Alloc>`. The default `std::allocator<T>` simply calls `operator new`/`operator delete`. A **minimal custom allocator** (C++11 and later; `std::allocator_traits` fills in the rest):

```cpp
template <class T>
struct ArenaAlloc {
    using value_type = T;
    Arena* arena;                                        // non-owning
    explicit ArenaAlloc(Arena* a) noexcept : arena(a) {}
    template <class U> ArenaAlloc(const ArenaAlloc<U>& o) noexcept : arena(o.arena) {}  // rebind
    T* allocate(std::size_t n) { return static_cast<T*>(arena->alloc(n * sizeof(T), alignof(T))); }
    void deallocate(T*, std::size_t) noexcept {}         // the arena frees everything at once
};
template <class T, class U>
bool operator==(const ArenaAlloc<T>& a, const ArenaAlloc<U>& b) { return a.arena == b.arena; }
template <class T, class U>
bool operator!=(const ArenaAlloc<T>& a, const ArenaAlloc<U>& b) { return !(a == b); }

std::vector<Order, ArenaAlloc<Order>> v{ArenaAlloc<Order>(&arena)};
```

A **bump (arena) allocator** behind it:

```cpp
class Arena {
    std::byte* buf_; size_t cap_, off_ = 0;
public:
    Arena(std::byte* b, size_t c) : buf_(b), cap_(c) {}
    void* alloc(size_t n, size_t align) {
        size_t p = (off_ + align - 1) & ~(align - 1);   // round up (align is a power of two)
        if (p + n > cap_) throw std::bad_alloc{};
        off_ = p + n; return buf_ + p;                  // O(1): add and compare
    }
    void reset() { off_ = 0; }                          // free EVERYTHING in O(1)
};
```

**C++17 polymorphic memory resources (`<memory_resource>`)** give you this without writing an allocator class:

```cpp
#include <memory_resource>
std::array<std::byte, 64 * 1024> buffer;                        // on the stack
std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource arena{buffer.data(), buffer.size()};
std::pmr::vector<Order> orders{&arena};                          // allocates from the buffer
orders.reserve(1000);                                            // no malloc as long as it fits
// other resources: unsynchronized_pool_resource (single-threaded pools by size class),
// synchronized_pool_resource, new_delete_resource(), null_memory_resource() (throws: catches leaks to the heap)
```

- `pmr` containers carry a `memory_resource*` (8 B) and call it through a **virtual** `do_allocate`. That is a small indirection cost, but one container type works with any resource.
- A monotonic resource never frees individual blocks (`deallocate` is a no-op). Memory is returned when the resource is destroyed or `release()`d, so it is perfect for per-message or per-batch scratch space.
- Replacing the global allocator (linking `tcmalloc` or `jemalloc`, or `LD_PRELOAD`) speeds up every `new` with no code change, but it is still not free. The hot path should not allocate at all.

