```cpp
template <typename T, size_t N>
class Pool {
    union Slot { Slot* next; alignas(T) unsigned char mem[sizeof(T)]; };
    std::array<Slot, N> slots_;          // one contiguous block, allocated once
    Slot* free_list = nullptr;
public:
    Pool() { for (auto& s : slots_) { s.next = free_list; free_list = &s; } }
    template <class... A> T* acquire(A&&... a) {
        if (!free_list) return nullptr;              // capacity was sized up front
        Slot* s = free_list; free_list = s->next;    // O(1) pop
        return new (s->mem) T(std::forward<A>(a)...);// placement new: construct in place
    }
    void release(T* p) {
        p->~T();                                     // explicit destructor call
        Slot* s = reinterpret_cast<Slot*>(p);
        s->next = free_list; free_list = s;          // O(1) push
    }
};
static Pool<Order, 1'000'000> pool;   // static storage: allocated and pre-faulted at start-up
```

Why it beats `new`/`delete`: acquire and release are O(1) pointer swaps (a few ns), with **no syscalls**, no allocator locks and no page faults after warm-up, so latency is **deterministic**. All slots are the same size, so there is **no fragmentation**, and the orders sit in one contiguous block, which is good for cache and TLB. The costs are a fixed capacity and that it is single-threaded as written (use one pool per thread).

