short.modelAnswer:
```cpp
class Arena {
    std::byte* buf_; size_t cap_; size_t off_ = 0;
public:
    Arena(std::byte* b, size_t cap) : buf_(b), cap_(cap) {}
    void* alloc(size_t n, size_t align) {          // align must be a power of two
        size_t p = (off_ + align - 1) & ~(align - 1);   // round the offset up to a multiple of align
        if (p + n > cap_) return nullptr;          // out of space
        off_ = p + n;
        return buf_ + p;
    }
    void reset() { off_ = 0; }                     // frees everything at once, O(1)
};
```

`(off + align - 1) & ~(align - 1)` rounds the offset up to the next multiple of `align`. For example, off = 13 and align = 8 gives (20) & ~7 = 16. That works because `align` is a power of two, so `~(align-1)` clears the low bits.

Allocation is an add, a mask and a compare: a few ns, with no syscalls or locks. There is **no individual free**, only `reset`. Destructors are not run, so it suits trivially destructible data, or you call them yourself. It is appropriate when many objects share one **lifetime**: per-message decode scratch, per-batch or per-tick temporary data, or start-up tables. It is wrong for objects with independent lifetimes, which need a pool.
